    "finalResponse": "A total solar eclipse requires the Moon to be in a \"new moon\" phase *and* positioned near one of the points where its orbit crosses Earth's orbital plane (a node), because the Moon's orbit is tilted about 5° relative to Earth's. Consequently, most new moons occur when the Moon is slightly above or below the Sun in the sky, preventing the alignment needed for an eclipse.\n\nThe remaining uncertainty lies in the precise timing of future node crossings relative to the lunar cycle, which determines exactly which new moons will result in eclipses; however, NASA's eclipse catalogs predict these events with high confidence based on well-understood orbital mechanics.\n\n**Source:** NASA Eclipse Web Site: [https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEcat/SEcat.html](https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/SEcat/SEcat.html)",
